# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE HUB IS NOT GIVEN THE TEAM'S SECRET. It is given a value DERIVED from it.
#
# Teammates share one master secret. From it, two things are derived:
#
#   ZEVET_TOKEN   = SHA-256("zevet-auth" || secret)   <- the hub gets this
#   document key  = HKDF(secret)                      <- the hub never sees it
#
# That asymmetry is the only reason the hub can relay collaborative editing
# traffic it genuinely cannot read. Putting the master secret in ZEVET_TOKEN
# hands the hub the document key and undoes the whole scheme, so do not.
#
# Derive the value for the line below with:
#
#   node -e 'import("./client/secret.mjs").then(m => console.log(m.deriveAuthToken(process.argv[1])))' <secret>
#
# `setup.sh` and `setup.ps1` print the same line at the end of a run, which is
# the easier way to get it. See "The master secret, and the cutover" in
# README.md, and the header of client/secret.mjs for what this does and does
# not protect against.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# HUB SIDE. The DERIVED token, 64 hex characters -- not the master secret.
ZEVET_TOKEN=derive-this-do-not-paste-the-master-secret-here

# Where the hub listens (hub side).
PORT=8787

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLIENT SIDE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The MASTER SECRET, the thing teammates actually share. `setup.sh` and
# `setup.ps1` normally write this into ~/.zevet/config.json and you never set it
# here; the variable exists so a container or a CI job can supply it without a
# config file. Hex, at least 24 bytes:  openssl rand -hex 24
ZEVET_SECRET=

# Where the hooks send events. No trailing slash.
ZEVET_HUB=http://127.0.0.1:8787

# Your display name in the lanes. Defaults to your OS username.
ZEVET_ACTOR=andrew

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEGACY, and it still works on purpose.
#
# Before the master secret, ~/.zevet/config.json held a raw `token` that WAS the
# hub's ZEVET_TOKEN, compared byte for byte. Installs like that keep
# authenticating -- right up until the hub's own ZEVET_TOKEN is changed to a
# derived value, at which point they get a 401 and have to re-run setup. There
# is deliberately no window in which the hub accepts both, because a hub that
# also accepted the master secret would, by definition, be holding it.
#
# A legacy install has no master secret, so there is nothing to derive a
# document key from and the editor is unavailable to it. `npm run doctor` says
# so in as many words.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
